<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hexham</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hexham is a market town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northumberland">Northumberland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-tyne">River Tyne</a>. Around 674 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilfrid">Wilfrid</a> founded a monastery there whose church, built with stone from Roman sites, was among the finest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> England; its crypt survives under the present Hexham Abbey. It was briefly a bishopric, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-heavenfield">Battle of Heavenfield</a> of 634 was fought nearby.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hexham.jpg" alt="Hexham" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Benjamin Smith · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hexham_-_Abbey_church_-_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Hexham stands on the south bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-tyne">River Tyne</a>, formed by the meeting of the North Tyne and South Tyne at Warden nearby, and close to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadrians-wall">Hadrian's Wall</a>. Newcastle upon Tyne lies 40 kilometres to the east and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlisle">Carlisle</a> 60 kilometres to the west. The name probably comes from the Old English Hagustaldes ea, the stream of the hagustald, a word related to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-high-german">Old High German</a> hagustalt for a younger son who takes land outside the settlement.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 671 or 672 the Northumbrian queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelthryth">Æthelthryth</a> gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilfrid">Saint Wilfrid</a> the site for a church at Hexham, and Hexham Abbey originated as the monastery Wilfrid founded in 674. Its crypt survives and incorporates stones taken from nearby Roman ruins, probably Corbridge or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadrians-wall">Hadrian's Wall</a>. King Ælfwald of the Northumbrians, murdered by Sicga on 23 September 788, was buried in the church.</p><p>The town suffered in the border wars between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. William Wallace burnt it in 1297, and in 1312 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-the-bruce">Robert the Bruce</a> demanded and received £2,000 from the town and monastery to spare them the same fate. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wars-of-the-roses">Wars of the Roses</a> the Battle of Hexham was fought in 1464 somewhere south of the town, and the defeated Lancastrian commander Henry Beaufort, 3rd Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somerset">Somerset</a>, was executed in the market place. In 1715 James Radclyffe, 3rd Earl of Derwentwater, raised the standard for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-francis-edward-stuart">James Francis Edward Stuart</a> there. In 1761 troops of the North York Militia fired on a crowd protesting about militia service, killing some 45 people.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Hexham is a market town and civil parish in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northumberland">Northumberland</a> with a population of 13,097 in 2011. Hexham Abbey, whose present church largely dates from about 1170 to 1250 in the Early English Gothic style, dominates the town. Nearby are the Shambles, built by Sir Walter Blackett in 1766, and the Moot Hall, a medieval courthouse. The Old Gaol, built between 1330 and 1333, was one of the first purpose-built jails in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. Hexham was long known for glove-making and leather dressing.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hexham” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The monasteries of Lindisfarne, Wearmouth-Jarrow, Whitby, <b>Hexham</b> and Ripon created the so-called Northumbrian Renaissance, which blended Irish, Anglo-Saxon and Mediterranean traditions.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-northumbria">Kingdom of Northumbria · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
